<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.13.3: Comparing Exponential Functions</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01484</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.13.3: Comparing Exponential Functions</md:title>
    <md:uuid>728abc90-76f2-44d2-990d-479e7646590b</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="practice" id="sect-00001">      <title>Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">Here are graphs representing two functions and descriptions of two functions.</para>
      <para id="para-00002">
          <media alt="Graph of two exponential functions, origin O. time(months) and area (square inches). ">
            <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_15_06_01.svg"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>
      <list id="list-00001">
        <item>Function <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>: The area of a wall that is covered by Mold A, in square inches, doubling every month.</item>
        <item>Function <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>: The area of a wall that is covered by Mold B, in square inches, tripling every month.</item>
      </list>
      
        <exercise id="exer-00001">
          <problem id="prob-00001">
            <para id="para-00003">Which graph represents each function? Explain your answer.</para>
            <para id="para-00004">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00001">
            <para id="para-00005">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00006">Since Mold B is growing more quickly, the graph that shows a greater growth factor (the one starting lower and
    ending higher) is function <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> (solid).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      
        <exercise id="exer-00002">
          <problem id="prob-00002">
            <para id="para-00007">When the mold was first spotted and measured, was there more of Mold A or Mold B? Explain how you know.</para>
            <para id="para-00008">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00002">
            <para id="para-00009">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00010">There was more of Mold A. The graph for <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> (dashed) shows a greater vertical intercept.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      
        <exercise id="exer-00003">
          <problem id="prob-00003">
            <para id="para-00011">What does the point <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>p</m:mi><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mi>q</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> tell us in this situation? Explain your answer.</para>
            <para id="para-00012">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00003">
            <para id="para-00013">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00014">After <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>p</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> months, there was the same amount of Mold A and Mold B on the wall (<m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>q</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> square inches), after which
    Mold B started to have larger area than Mold A.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
    </section>
    <note class="self-check" id="note-00001">
      <exercise id="exer-00005">
        <problem id="prob-00005">
        <para id="para-00015"><link class="os-embed" url="#exercise/Alg1_5_13_3_SC"/></para>
        </problem>
        </exercise>
        </note>
        <section id="sect-00002">
          <title>Additional Resources</title>
          
    <section id="sect-00003">
      <title>Reading Exponential Graphs</title>
      <para id="para-00016">The graphs of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> are located below and are functions of the form <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:msup><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
      <para id="para-00017">
          <media alt="GRAPH OF EXPONENTIAL GROWTH FUNCTIONS, F AND G. FUNCTION G HAS A SMALLER \(y\)-intercepts AND CLIMBS MORE QUICKLY THAN FUNCTION F.">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_15_08_01.jpg"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>
      <para id="para-00018">Which curve has the larger <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>a</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> value?</para>
      <para id="para-00019"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> because its <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept is higher.</para>
      <para id="para-00020">Which curve has the larger <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> value?</para>
      <para id="para-00021"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> because it is growing more quickly.</para>
      <note class="try" id="note-00002">
  <title>Try It: Reading Exponential Graphs</title>
      <para id="para-00022">The graphs of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> are located below and are functions of the form <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:msup><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
      <para id="para-00023">
          <media alt="GRAPH OF EXPONENTIAL GROWTH FUNCTIONS, F AND G. FUNCTION F HAS A SMALLER \(y\)-intercepts AND CLIMBS MORE QUICKLY THAN FUNCTION G.">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_15_08_02.jpg"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>
      
        <exercise id="exer-00004">
          <problem id="prob-00004">
            <para id="para-00024">Which curve has the larger <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> value?</para>
            <para id="para-00025">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00004">
            <para id="para-00026">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00027"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> has the larger <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> value because it is growing faster than <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </note>
    </section>
        </section>
        
  </content>
